function softHTML(){ const c=chain(); return `<div class="grid2">${card('Signal path for the selected card', stageTable(c.stages))}${card('Level diagram', levelSVG(c.stages))}</div>${card('Wiring path, step by step', `<ol class="small"><li>POT #${D.sel+1} (10K LINEAR, CW end up)</li><li>1 ft cable: SH, BK, RD</li><li>EDAC 38-pin plug pins ${PINS[D.sel].join(', ')}</li><li>Customer wiring (up to 1000 ft)</li><li>TB8202 behind card ${D.sel+1}: REM IN HIGH / WIPER / LOW</li><li>M8202 REM GAIN jumper IN → VCA ±15 dB about the centre (after RANGE / OFFSET set-up)</li></ol>`)}`; }
const findings=[
  `<b>Artwork ${SIM.year(2026)}: none for the panel itself.</b> No CorelDRAW, overlay or silk file in the archive draws the M8202RC face; the panel stays drawn from the DWGs, with its layout inferred. The far end of every pot is drawn: the REM IN corner of ${SIM.src(TR2_SRC,'M8202TR2.CDR')} (2005-01-20), the “M8202 TERMINAL OPTIONS” sheet, now sits under the wiring drawings.`,
  `<b>The sheet agrees on the pot.</b> M8202TR2 draws a “10 kΩ LINEAR” pot with a CW arrow, the same 10K LINEAR part (WBS 10-080) that RC1 calls for. The M8208’s page in ${SIM.src(MASTER_SRC,'the 2011 master')} draws a 5 kΩ pot on the same TB8202.`,
  `<b>The sheet’s REM IN order is flipped.</b> M8202TR2 lists the three REM IN screws as LOW, WIPER, HIGH from the top; the TB8202 copper puts HIGH on the top screw. An installer working from the sheet would wire the pot backwards, so the gain would rise as the knob turns anticlockwise <span class="tag-inf">inferred</span>.`,
  `<b>Twelve pots for a twelve-slot frame.</b> RC1: “12 POTS ARE HOUSED ON EACH PANEL”; RC2 maps POT #1–#12 to 36 of the plug's 38 contacts. One panel serves a full MF82/12 of M8202s.`,
  `<b>The cable shield carries the HIGH end.</b> RC1 routes SH → EDAC C → REM IN HIGH, BK → B → WIPER and RD → A → LOW. Using the shield as a signal conductor is unusual; with a DC control voltage it works, but it leaves the pair unscreened.`,
  `<b>Two contacts unused.</b> W (the centre contact) and TT are not inside any POT outline on RC2; RC2 also has a “SHIELD PIN” callout with its own small circle beside the plug body.`,
  `<b>The panel face is not drawn.</b> Both DWGs are wiring drawings; there is no front-panel artwork for the M8202-RC in the archive, so the panel here is an inferred layout.`,
  `<b>The README's pairing is right, but the B uses it too.</b> The panel feeds REM IN on the TB8202 — the M8202 and the 600 Ω M8202B (TB8202/600) share it.`,
  `<b>RC1 has a backup.</b> <span class="mono">M8202RC1.BAK</span> is an earlier save of the same drawing.`,
];
